A||||8||pHoOl||Tire pressure||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||06-22-2000||10:57 PM||||I think that I was able to read 30 PSI from the sticker on the door.  The dirt was quite thick though.  But with gas being so expensive here in Michigan (2.159 per gallon)  I'm thinking of pumping the tires up for better gas mileage.  Does anybody know how much you can inflate beyond the 30 before you start running into being "over inflated" and getting the annoying wear only in the middle?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Megafiero||06-22-2000||11:07 PM||||Well  your best bet is to look at the sidewall of your tire and see what it says for the max presure.  If your tires are not stock, then what the inside of your door says may not apply.  And to keep from getting added tire ware,  just don't go past the max psi.  well GL,  and I hear ya on them MI gas prices,  there a killer,  I'm paying 2.21 for 92 =(||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||06-22-2000||11:29 PM||||Boy you guys are making the gas co's rich right now. Here in L.A. reg is $1.65 gal. Of course we usually are paying more than anywhere else. What's up with this?<P>I always run 35 lbs in mine with no over inflation wear. You can probably go higher, but 35 is safe for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-23-2000||02:08 AM||||Max tire pressure is dependent on the tires. My Euro T/A's have a max of 50 and I have them set at 45. My original regular T/A radials had a max of 35 and I kept them at 32. Go by what's on the tire.<P>By the way Gas in Chicago is $2.17 for 87 octane, $2.25 for 89 octane and $2.33 for 93 Octane. We are the highest average in the country!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||06-23-2000||07:14 AM||||If you're getting premium for 2.21, you're getting a steal!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-23-2000||07:27 PM||||The stock 195/whateverx14 tires GM originally put on allot of these are 30psi. I run the ones I have now at 33. (195/70x14 sears/coopers) if you go to other sizes you'll need to tweek it a bit one way or the other. <P>Tires change constantly. not just in improved manufacturing but over the life of the tire. There is no exact number to give for a tire. You should always start at or close to OEM spec and tweek as needed for the specific tires, car, and driving conditions. Usually you only need +-2-5PSI change.<P>Never exceed max inflations printed on the tires. Don't assume max inflation is the best number, it's just the max. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||06-23-2000||10:00 PM||||I'm running stock size Pirelli tires(max 48psi) on my Formula, and I must run 38psi or they wear out the outside edges quickly from underinflation. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||06-23-2000||11:33 PM||||That sounds a little strange, 48 psi.  Are these tire for a truck or something?  I know those Euro TAs are freaky tires or should I say tyres.  I drove a GTI with those things on it, and I swear they looked under inflated at 35 psi even under that light little box of a car.  They must have weak sidewalls or something.  I didn't even like the way they handled.  Way too greasy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||06-23-2000||11:48 PM||||If the car you drove had Euro T/A's and only 35psi then yes it is under inflated and will handle "greasy" because the tire will heat up and the sidewall will roll under in hard cornering. The reinforced sidewalls and the large block design on the tread allow the higher air pressure. I have "tested" the handling capabilities of my car and it sticks like glue. ||||reg||1
